/**
 * @defgroup triqs-atom-diag Atomic problem diagonalization
 * @brief Exact diagonalization of atomic (local) Hamiltonians and their atomic Green's function.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-atom-diag module performs exact diagonalization of a many-body Hamiltonian \f$ \hat H \f$
 * acting on the Fock space of a finite set of fermionic single-particle states. The Hilbert space is split into
 * invariant subspaces of \f$ \hat H \f$, each of which is diagonalized independently:
 * \f[
 *   H_B = U_B \, \mathrm{diag}(E_B) \, U^{\dagger}_B \; ,
 * \f]
 * where \f$ B \f$ indexes the subspaces, \f$ H_B \f$ is the Hamiltonian restricted to the subspace, \f$ U_B \f$ is the
 * unitary matrix mapping the Fock basis of the subspace to the eigenbasis of the Hamiltonian, and \f$ E_B \f$ is the
 * vector of eigenvalues. By convention the global ground-state energy is subtracted at construction, so that the
 * smallest eigenvalue across all subspaces is zero.
 *
 * The invariant subspaces are built either by an auto-partition procedure, by an explicit list of quantum-number
 * operators, or by a particle-number window, and each block is then diagonalized with the QR algorithm.
 *
 * **Notation used throughout the API:**
 *
 * - \f$ B,\, B' \f$ : subspace index (parameter `sp_index`),
 * - \f$ \dim(B) \f$ : dimension of subspace \f$ B \f$, returned by triqs::atom_diag::atom_diag::get_subspace_dim,
 * - \f$ E_{B,i} \f$ : the \f$ i \f$-th eigenvalue inside subspace \f$ B \f$,
 * - \f$ N \f$ : dimension of the full Hilbert space.
 *
 * **Atomic Green's function.**
 *
 * Derived from a solved diagonalization problem, the atomic Green's function is built in the Lehmann (spectral)
 * representation,
 * \f[
 *   G_{ab}(z) = \frac{1}{Z} \sum_{B, B'} \sum_{i=0}^{\dim(B)-1} \sum_{j=0}^{\dim(B')-1} \Bigl( e^{-\beta E_{B,i}} +
 *   \eta\, e^{-\beta E_{B',j}} \Bigr) \frac{\langle B, i\,|\, \hat c_a \,|\, B', j\rangle \, \langle B', j\,|\,
 *   \hat c_b^\dagger \,|\, B, i\rangle} {z + E_{B,i} - E_{B',j}} \; ,
 * \f]
 * where \f$ \eta = +1 \f$ for fermionic and \f$ \eta = -1 \f$ for bosonic statistics, \f$ Z \f$ is the partition
 * function, \f$ B, B' \f$ are subspace indices, and \f$ i, j \f$ are eigenstate indices inside the corresponding
 * subspaces with \f$ 0 \le i < \dim(B) \f$ and \f$ 0 \le j < \dim(B') \f$. The orbital indices \f$ a, b \f$ run over
 * one Green's-function block and are mapped to fundamental-operator linear indices through the fundamental operator
 * set. The only non-zero contributions come from \f$ (B, B') \f$ pairs that are connected by the application of a
 * creation/annihilation operator, i.e. for which the corresponding matrix block is non-zero.
 *
 * The poles
 * \f[
 *   p = E_{B',j} - E_{B,i}
 * \f]
 * and the residues
 * \f[
 *   r_p = \bigl(e^{-\beta E_{B,i}} + \eta\, e^{-\beta E_{B',j}}\bigr)
 *   \langle B, i\,|\, \hat c_a \,|\, B', j\rangle \langle B', j\,|\, \hat c_b^\dagger \,|\, B, i\rangle / Z
 * \f]
 * are first stored in the Lehmann data structure, then evaluated on the requested target mesh (imaginary time,
 * Matsubara, Legendre, or real frequency).
 *
 * The solver itself is exposed through triqs::atom_diag::atom_diag (with real- and complex-valued specializations);
 * a set of free functions builds thermodynamic averages, quantum-number tables and the atomic Green's function on
 * different meshes from a solved instance.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-detmanip Determinant manipulation
 * @brief Manipulating determinants efficiently.
 *
 * @details The weight of a MC configuration in CTQMC solvers is often given in terms of the determinant of one or
 * more matrices such that the acceptance probability is a ratio of these determinants. It is therefore important to
 * be able to efficiently compute these determinants and ratios of determinants.
 *
 * Let \f$ F^{(n)} \f$ be the \f$ n \times n \f$ matrix that we are interested in. We assume that the elements of the
 * matrix can be written as \f$ F^{(n)}_{ij} = f(x_i, y_j) \f$, where \f$ f \f$ is a triqs::det_manip::MatrixBuilder
 * object and \f$ \mathbf{x} \f$ and \f$ \mathbf{y} \f$ are vectors of size \f$ n \f$ that contain the arguments for
 * \f$ f \f$ and therefore determine the contents of the matrix.
 *
 * In the following, we will not work directly with the matrix \f$ F^{(n)} \f$ but with \f$ G^{(n)} \f$ which has its
 * rows and columns permuted w.r.t. \f$ F^{(n)} \f$, i.e. \f$ F^{(n)} = P^{(n)}_r G^{(n)} P^{(n)}_c \f$. \f$ P^{(n)}_r
 * \f$ and \f$ P^{(n)}_c \f$ are permutation matrices that permute the rows and columns of \f$ G^{(n)} \f$ to restore
 * the original matrix. This gives us more flexibility when adding, removing, or modifying rows and columns.
 *
 * Suppose we know the matrix \f$ G^{(n)} \f$ and its determinant \f$ \det(G^{(n)}) \f$ and that we want to add \f$ k
 * \f$ new rows and columns to the matrix. We can write the resulting matrix as
 * \f[
 *   G^{(n+k)} = \begin{bmatrix} G^{(n)} & B \\ C & D \end{bmatrix} =
 *   \begin{bmatrix} [M^{(n)}]^{-1} & B \\ C & D \end{bmatrix} =
 *   [M^{(n+k)}]^{-1} \; .
 * \f]
 * Here, we have introduced the inverse matrix \f$ M^{(n)} = [G^{(n)}]^{-1} \f$ and the matrices \f$ B \f$, \f$ C \f$,
 * and \f$ D \f$ which are of size \f$ n \times k \f$, \f$ k \times n \f$, and \f$ k \times k \f$, respectively. They
 * contain the elements of the new rows and columns.
 *
 * By making use of the block structure of the matrix (see also <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Block_matrix">
 * Wikipedia</a>), the inverse matrix takes the form
 * \f[
 *   M^{(n+k)} = \begin{bmatrix} M^{(n)} + M^{(n)} B (D - C M^{(n)} B)^{-1} C M^{(n)} & -M^{(n)} B (D - C M^{(n)}
 *   B)^{-1} \\ -(D - C M^{(n)} B)^{-1} C M^{(n)} & (D - C M^{(n)} B)^{-1} \end{bmatrix} =
 *   \begin{bmatrix} P & Q \\ R & S \end{bmatrix}  \; ,
 * \f]
 * and its determinant can be computed as
 * \f[
 *   \det(G^{(n+k)}) = \det(G^{(n)}) \det(D - C M^{(n)} B) = \det(G^{(n)}) \det(S^{-1}) \; .
 * \f]
 * Here, \f$ P \f$, \f$ Q \f$, \f$ R \f$, and \f$ S \f$ are matrices of size \f$ n \times n \f$, \f$ n \times k \f$,
 * \f$ k \times n \f$, and \f$ k \times k \f$, respectively.
 *
 * The original matrix \f$ F^{(n)} \f$ and its determinant can be obtained with \f$ F^{(n)} = P^{(n)}_r G^{(n)}
 * P^{(n)}_c \f$ and \f$ \det(F^{(n)}) = \det(P^{(n)}_r) \det(G^{(n)}) det(P^{(n)}_c) = s^{(n)} \det(G^{(n)}) \f$,
 * where \f$ s^{(n)} = \det(P^{(n)}_r) det(P^{(n)}_c) = \pm 1 \f$ is a sign associated with the permutation matrices.
 *
 * So by keeping track of the inverse matrix \f$ M^{(n)} \f$, the determinant \f$ \det(G^{(n)}) \f$ and two additional
 * permutation matrices that specify how the rows and columns have been permuted w.r.t. the original matrix, we can
 * add, remove, or modify (a few) rows and columns fairly efficiently.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-experimental Experimental tools
 * @brief Experimental tools that are still under development and whose API may change.
 *
 * @details They live in the `triqs::experimental` namespace and are organized into two submodules:
 * 
 * - @ref triqs-experimental-lattice provides advanced lattice tools.
 * - @ref triqs-experimental-utility provides numerical helpers.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-experimental
 * @defgroup triqs-experimental-lattice Lattice tools (experimental)
 * @brief Fourier polynomials, tight-binding Hamiltonians, superlattices, Wannier90 loaders and Brillouin-zone 
 * integration.
 *
 * @details These tools represent lattice functions \f$ f(\mathbf{k}) = \sum_\mathbf{R} f(\mathbf{R}) e^{2 \pi i
 * \mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{R}} \f$ as Fourier polynomials and build tight-binding Hamiltonians on top of them. They
 * provide band-structure calculations, folding onto a superlattice, loading of Wannier90 `*_tb.dat` / `*_hr.dat` files, 
 * and the computation of the non-interacting local Green's function via Brillouin-zone integration.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-experimental
 * @defgroup triqs-experimental-utility Utilities (experimental)
 * @brief Adaptive 1D integration, a generic CLEF integrator wrapper and root-finding routines.
 *
 * @details These utilities provide the numerical building blocks used by the experimental 
 * @ref triqs-experimental-lattice tools: an adaptive 13-point Kronrod 1D integrator, a generic wrapper that integrates 
 * CLEF expressions over a placeholder, and dichotomy/bisection root finding.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs Green's functions
 * @brief Generic containers for Green's functions, self energies and other important functions in quantum many-body
 * physics.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-gfs in the **TRIQS** library are generic containers for functions which are not known
 * analytically. As the name suggests, they are intended to be used for quantum many-body Green's functions, self
 * energies or other correlation functions, but they can in principle be used for more general functions.
 *
 * The central container is triqs::gfs::gf, a class template parameterised by two ingredients:
 * - a **mesh** (see @ref triqs-meshes) that fixes the mathematical domain of the function, how it is represented in
 * memory and how it is evaluated, and
 * - a **target**, which fixes the value stored at each mesh point: a scalar (triqs::gfs::scalar_valued), a matrix
 * (triqs::gfs::matrix_valued) or a higher-rank tensor (triqs::gfs::tensor_valued). The target types live in
 * @ref triqs-gfs-targets.
 *
 * Each container comes with non-owning view classes, triqs::gfs::gf_view and triqs::gfs::gf_const_view, that refer to
 * the data of an existing Green's function without copying it. The generic requirements shared by all of these types
 * are spelled out as C++20 concepts in @ref triqs-gfs-concepts.
 *
 * Many physical problems are naturally block-diagonal (e.g. in spin or orbital sectors). triqs::gfs::block_gf and its
 * view triqs::gfs::block_gf_view represent a vector of Green's functions sharing a common type, while
 * triqs::gfs::block2_gf provides the two-index analogue.
 *
 * Around these containers the module provides a library of physical operations such as density and high-frequency tail
 * evaluation (@ref triqs-gfs-functions), HDF5 serialisation (@ref triqs-gfs-io), transforms between representations
 * such as the Fourier transform and the Padé analytic continuation (@ref triqs-gfs-transforms), and factory functions,
 * expression templates and other utilities (@ref triqs-gfs-utils).
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-concepts GF concepts
 * @brief Concepts that specify the requirements for Green's function types.
 *
 * @details The Green's function machinery is generic: any type satisfying the relevant concept can be used with the
 * algorithms in @ref triqs-gfs. The concepts in this group spell out the minimal interface those types must expose,
 * most prominently triqs::gfs::MemoryGf, which constrains a type to own a mesh and a compatible data array.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-containers GF containers
 * @brief Green's function container and view types.
 *
 * @details This group contains the central Green's function containers and their non-owning views:
 * - triqs::gfs::gf is the owning container, parameterised by a mesh and a target.
 * - triqs::gfs::gf_view and triqs::gfs::gf_const_view are mutable and constant views into the data of an existing
 * Green's function.
 *
 * The block-diagonal containers are collected in @ref triqs-gfs-block, and the target value types in
 * @ref triqs-gfs-targets.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-containers
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-block Block Green's functions
 * @brief Block-diagonal Green's function containers.
 *
 * @details Many physical problems are naturally block-diagonal (e.g. in spin or orbital sectors).
 * triqs::gfs::block_gf and its view triqs::gfs::block_gf_view represent a vector of Green's functions grouped into
 * named blocks, while triqs::gfs::block2_gf and triqs::gfs::block2_gf_view provide the two-index analogue. The block
 * structure of a Green's function is described by triqs::gfs::gf_struct_t.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-containers
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-targets GF targets
 * @brief Target types that fix the value stored at each mesh point of a Green's function.
 *
 * @details A target determines the value stored at each mesh point of a Green's function: a scalar
 * (triqs::gfs::scalar_valued), a matrix (triqs::gfs::matrix_valued) or a higher-rank tensor
 * (triqs::gfs::tensor_valued), each available in a real- and a complex-valued variant. This group also contains the
 * traits that map between a (scalar type, rank) pair and the corresponding target type.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-utils GF utilities
 * @brief Operations and utilities acting on Green's function containers.
 *
 * @details This is a parent group collecting the building blocks of the Green's function module, organised by purpose:
 * construction (@ref triqs-gfs-factories), lazy arithmetic (@ref triqs-gfs-algebra), evaluation and interpolation
 * (@ref triqs-gfs-evaluation), MPI support (@ref triqs-gfs-mpi), CLEF auto-assignment (@ref triqs-gfs-clef), data
 * reshaping and slicing (@ref triqs-gfs-reshape), block mapping (@ref triqs-gfs-map) and symmetry reduction
 * (@ref triqs-gfs-symmetry).
 * 
 * General utilities are placed directly in @ref triqs-gfs-utils.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-factories GF factories
 * @brief Factory functions that construct Green's function containers.
 *
 * @details Convenience factories that build triqs::gfs::gf, triqs::gfs::block_gf and triqs::gfs::block2_gf objects (and
 * their views) from meshes, shapes, block structures or existing Green's functions.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-algebra GF algebra
 * @brief Lazy expression templates and arithmetic operations on Green's functions.
 *
 * @details Arithmetic on Green's functions is *lazy*: an expression such as `g1 + g2` or `2 * g` does not compute a
 * new Green's function immediately. Instead it builds a lightweight expression node (triqs::gfs::gf_expr for binary
 * operations, triqs::gfs::gf_unary_m_expr for the unary minus) that models the `GreenFunction` concept and is only 
 * evaluated element by element when the expression is assigned to a container or accessed. This fuses chained 
 * operations and avoids intermediate temporaries.
 *
 * The operators are generated by macros, so they do not appear individually in the member list below. The complete set
 * of supported operations is, with `g`, `g1`, `g2` Green's functions and `s` a scalar (an integer, real or complex
 * number):
 *
 * **Binary operators** (build a lazy triqs::gfs::gf_expr):
 * - `g1 + g2`, `g1 - g2` — element-wise addition / subtraction of two Green's functions.
 * - `g1 * g2`, `g1 / g2` — element-wise product / quotient of two Green's functions.
 * - `s * g`, `g * s` — multiplication by a scalar from either side.
 * - `s / g`, `g / s` — scalar divided by a Green's function, and Green's function divided by a scalar.
 * - Note: addition and subtraction with a bare scalar are **not** available as binary expressions (only `*` and `/`
 *   accept a scalar operand); use the in-place `+=` / `-=` operators below instead.
 *
 * **Unary operator:**
 * - `-g` — negation (builds a lazy triqs::gfs::gf_unary_m_expr).
 *
 * **Compound assignment** (in-place, defined for both triqs::gfs::gf and triqs::gfs::gf_view and implemented as
 * `g = g OP x`):
 * - `g += x`, `g -= x`, `g *= x`, `g /= x`, where `x` is another Green's function or a scalar.
 *
 * **In-place operations with a matrix or scalar:**
 * - `g += m`, `g -= m` — add / subtract a matrix `m` at every mesh point of a matrix-valued Green's function.
 * - `g += s`, `g -= s` — for a matrix-valued Green's function, add / subtract `s` times the identity matrix at every
 *   mesh point; for a scalar-valued Green's function, add / subtract the scalar `s` directly.
 *
 * **Compatibility rules** enforced when combining two Green's functions:
 * - The meshes must be identical, otherwise a `triqs::runtime_error` is thrown; the data and target shapes must match
 *   as well (a scalar operand adopts the shape of the other operand).
 * - For products and quotients on an imaginary-time mesh (triqs::mesh::imtime, triqs::mesh::dlr_imtime) the particle
 *   statistics of the result is the parity sum of the operand statistics (e.g. fermion \f$ \times \f$ fermion
 *   \f$ \to \f$ boson).
 * - On a product mesh (triqs::mesh::prod) the rules are applied component by component.
 *
 * Block Green's functions (triqs::gfs::block_gf) support the analogous set of arithmetic operations, applied
 * block by block.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-evaluation GF evaluation
 * @brief Evaluation, interpolation and mesh/target introspection of Green's functions.
 *
 * @details Tools to evaluate a Green's function at arbitrary points using the interpolation scheme of its mesh
 * (triqs::gfs::gf_evaluator, triqs::gfs::default_evaluator) and to introspect its mesh and target
 * (triqs::gfs::get_mesh, triqs::gfs::target_value_t).
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-mpi GF MPI support
 * @brief MPI broadcast and reduction of Green's functions.
 *
 * @details MPI-aware operations for the Green's function containers: triqs::gfs::mpi_broadcast and
 * triqs::gfs::mpi_reduce (with the in-place variant triqs::gfs::mpi_reduce_into), for both single and block Green's
 * functions.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-clef CLEF auto-assignment
 * @brief CLEF integration enabling the `g(w_) << expr` assignment syntax.
 *
 * @details Hooks that let a Green's function be filled by a lazy CLEF expression, e.g. `g(w_) << 1 / (w_ - eps)`, by
 * evaluating the expression at every mesh point.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-reshape GF reshaping and slicing
 * @brief Reshape, flatten and slice the data of a Green's function.
 *
 * @details Utilities that reinterpret the data of a Green's function: flattening the target into a two-dimensional
 * form (triqs::gfs::flatten_gf_2d), slicing the target space, transposing, and forming restricted or reinterpreted
 * views.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-map GF block mapping
 * @brief Apply a function block-by-block on block Green's functions.
 *
 * @details Helpers that lift a function acting on a single Green's function to a function acting on each block of a
 * block Green's function (triqs::gfs::map_block_gf).
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-utils
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-symmetry GF symmetries
 * @brief Symmetry-reduced representation of Green's functions.
 *
 * @details Tools to exploit symmetries of a Green's function by storing and reconstructing only a symmetry-reduced set
 * of data points.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-io GF input/output
 * @brief HDF5 serialisation of Green's functions.
 *
 * @details This group provides the HDF5 read and write support for the Green's function containers, so that they can
 * be stored to and restored from disk.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-functions GF functions
 * @brief Physical operations acting on Green's functions.
 *
 * @details This is a parent group for operations frequently applied to Green's functions: computing the particle
 * density (@ref triqs-gfs-density), fitting and manipulating high-frequency tails (@ref triqs-gfs-tailfitting),
 * checking reality and hermiticity (@ref triqs-gfs-reality) and converting between basis representations
 * (@ref triqs-gfs-basis).
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-functions
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-density GF density
 * @brief Computation of the particle density from a Green's function.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-functions
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-tailfitting GF tail fitting
 * @brief Fitting and manipulation of the high-frequency tail of a Green's function.
 *
 * @details Tools to extract the high-frequency moments of a Green's function (triqs::gfs::fit_tail,
 * triqs::gfs::fit_hermitian_tail) and to construct or substitute tails.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-functions
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-reality GF reality and hermiticity
 * @brief Reality and hermiticity checks and operations on Green's functions.
 *
 * @details Predicates such as triqs::gfs::is_gf_real and triqs::gfs::is_gf_hermitian, together with related
 * operations such as complex conjugation.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-functions
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-basis GF basis conversions
 * @brief Conversions between basis representations of a Green's function.
 *
 * @details Conversions to and from compact basis representations such as Legendre polynomials and the discrete
 * Lehmann representation (DLR).
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-transforms GF transforms
 * @brief Transforms between different Green's function representations.
 *
 * @details This is a parent group for the transforms that map a Green's function from one representation to another:
 * the Fourier transform (@ref triqs-gfs-fourier), the Legendre/Matsubara transform (@ref triqs-gfs-legendre-matsubara)
 * and the Padé analytic continuation (@ref triqs-gfs-pade).
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-transforms
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-fourier GF Fourier transform
 * @brief Fourier transform between time and frequency representations.
 *
 * @details The Fourier transform between imaginary time and Matsubara frequency and between real time and real
 * frequency, as well as the lattice Fourier transform.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-transforms
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-legendre-matsubara GF Legendre-Matsubara transform
 * @brief Transform between the Legendre and Matsubara representations.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-gfs-transforms
 * @defgroup triqs-gfs-pade GF Padé continuation
 * @brief Padé analytic continuation from Matsubara to real frequencies.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-hilbert Hilbert space
 * @brief Fock state representations and Hilbert space tools for many-body systems.
 *
 * @details In the following, we introduce some basic concepts and notations used throughout the documentation.
 *
 * Let us start by considering a single particle Hilbert space \f$ \mathcal{H}_1 \f$ of finite dimension \f$ N \f$ with
 * a complete set of eigenstates \f$ \{ \lvert \alpha_i \rangle \}_{i=0}^{N-1} \f$.
 *
 * We can construct the associated Fock space \f$ \mathcal{F} \f$ as the Hilbert space spanned by all possible
 * occupation number configurations of the single particle states. Since we are only considering fermions, each single
 * particle state \f$ \lvert \alpha_i \rangle \f$ can either be empty or singly occupied, i.e. \f$ n_{\alpha_i} \in
 * \{0, 1\} \f$, which implies that \f$ \dim(\mathcal{F}) = 2^N \f$.
 *
 * The occupation number basis states of the Fock space are therefore given by
 * \f[
 *   \lvert n_{\alpha_0}, n_{\alpha_1}, \dots, n_{\alpha_{N-1}} \rangle = \prod_{i=0}^{N-1} (c_{\alpha_i}^\dagger)^{n_{
 *   \alpha_i}} \lvert 0 \rangle \; ,
 * \f]
 * where \f$ c_{\alpha_i}^\dagger \f$ is the fermionic creation operator such that \f$ \lvert \alpha_i \rangle =
 * c_{\alpha_i}^\dagger \lvert 0 \rangle \f$, and \f$ \lvert 0 \rangle \f$ is the vacuum state with no particles.
 *
 * If we restrict ourselves to \f$ N < 64 \f$, we can 1-to-1 map each occupation number basis state \f$ \lvert
 * n_{\alpha_0}, n_{\alpha_1}, \dots, n_{\alpha_{N-1}} \rangle \f$ to a single 64-bit integer
 * \f[
 *   f(n_{\alpha_0}, n_{\alpha_1}, \dots, n_{\alpha_{N-1}}) = \sum_{i=0}^{N-1} n_{\alpha_i} 2^i \; ,
 * \f]
 * where the \f$ i \f$-th bit of \f$ f \f$ is set if and only if the single particle state \f$ \lvert \alpha_i \rangle
 * \f$ is occupied.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-lattice Lattice tools
 * @brief Tools for working with Bravais lattices, Brillouin zones and tight-binding Hamiltonians.
 *
 * @details The lattice tools provide classes to describe crystal lattices in real and reciprocal space, and to build
 * tight-binding Hamiltonians on top of them. The main classes are:
 * - triqs::lattice::bravais_lattice: A Bravais lattice in 1, 2 or 3 dimensions, defined by a set of basis vectors and
 * the positions of (optionally named) atomic orbitals within the unit cell.
 * - triqs::lattice::brillouin_zone: The first Brillouin zone of a given Bravais lattice, defined by the corresponding
 * reciprocal-space basis vectors.
 * - triqs::lattice::tight_binding: A tight-binding Hamiltonian on a Bravais lattice, parameterised by lattice
 * displacements and the associated hopping (overlap) matrices between orbitals. It provides the Fourier transform to
 * reciprocal space, dispersion (band-structure) calculations on Brillouin zone meshes and integration with the TRIQS
 * Green's function framework.
 * - triqs::lattice::grid_generator: A forward iterator that produces a regular grid of points inside the d-dimensional
 * unit cube, useful for sampling the Brillouin zone.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-mc MC tools
 * @brief Tools to implement and perform Monte Carlo simulations.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-mc module provides a generic framework for writing Markov-chain Monte Carlo simulations. It
 * does not implement any particular physical algorithm; instead it supplies the scaffolding common to all such
 * simulations — driving the Markov chain, dispatching user-defined updates, accumulating user-defined observables, and
 * aggregating results across MPI ranks.
 *
 * A simulation is described by three ingredients supplied by the user:
 * - a **sign type** (real or complex, see triqs::mc_tools::DoubleOrComplex) that encodes the per-configuration weight,
 * - a set of **moves** that propose updates of the Markov chain and report their Metropolis acceptance ratio (see
 * @ref triqs-mc-moves and triqs::mc_tools::MCMove),
 * - a set of **measurements** that accumulate observables along the chain (see @ref triqs-mc-measures and
 * triqs::mc_tools::MCMeasure).
 *
 * These ingredients are plugged into a triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic instance, which manages *warmup* (equilibration,
 * optional move calibration) and *accumulation* (measurement) phases, organised into cycles of MC steps. The driver is
 * MPI-aware: random seeds, exception propagation, cycle counters and measurement reduction are all coordinated through
 * an `mpi::communicator`, so the same simulation code runs serially or in parallel.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-concepts MC concepts
 * @brief Concepts that specify the requirements for MC moves and measurements.
 *
 * @details The MC framework is generic: any user type that satisfies the relevant concept can be plugged into
 * triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic. The concepts in this group spell out the minimal interface those types must expose:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::DoubleOrComplex constrains the *sign type* to either `double` or `std::complex<double>`.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::MCMove requires `attempt()` and `accept()` returning the sign type, with optional `reject()`,
 * `calibrate()` and `collect_statistics()` hooks for adaptive moves and diagnostics.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::MCMeasure requires `accumulate(sign)` and `collect_results(mpi::communicator)`, leaving the
 * storage of partial accumulators entirely to the user.
 *
 * Because the framework relies on concepts rather than virtual inheritance, user moves and measurements remain plain,
 * value-semantic types; the triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic engine wraps them through type erasure internally.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-measures MC measurements
 * @brief Abstraction for Monte Carlo measurements.
 *
 * @details A *measurement* is any object that, given the current sign of the Markov chain, contributes to one or more
 * running estimators. This group provides the type-erased wrappers used by triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic to store
 * heterogeneous measurements together:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::measure wraps a user object satisfying triqs::mc_tools::MCMeasure and bookkeeps the number of
 * measurements, optional timing and an optional `report()` callback.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::measure_set is the container of registered measurements; it forwards `accumulate` and
 * `collect_results` to every measurement it owns.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::measure_aux holds *auxiliary* callables that are run on every cycle but do not themselves
 * accumulate an estimator (e.g. live diagnostics or periodic state dumps).
 *
 * Final reduction across MPI ranks is delegated to the user code inside `collect_results`, which receives the
 * communicator.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-moves MC moves
 * @brief Abstraction for Monte Carlo moves.
 *
 * @details A *move* proposes an update of the Markov chain and returns the Metropolis acceptance ratio. The framework
 * supplies two type-erased containers:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::move wraps a user object satisfying triqs::mc_tools::MCMove, tracks per-move proposal and
 * acceptance counters, optional timing, and forwards the optional `reject`, `calibrate` and `collect_statistics` hooks.
 * - triqs::mc_tools::move_set combines several moves with relative weights and itself satisfies the MCMove concept: on
 * each step it draws one of its sub-moves with probability proportional to its weight, allowing composite update
 * schemes to be assembled hierarchically.
 *
 * Acceptance ratios and timing statistics are exposed for diagnostics and are aggregated across MPI ranks when results
 * are collected.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-simulation MC simulation
 * @brief Run and manage MC simulations.
 *
 * @details This group contains triqs::mc_tools::mc_generic, the driver that ties together moves, measurements and the
 * random generator. A typical lifecycle is:
 * 1. construct an `mc_generic<S>` with a chosen engine name and seed,
 * 2. register moves with `add_move`, measurements with `add_measure`, and any auxiliary callables with
 * `add_measure_aux`,
 * 3. run `warmup` (equilibrate the chain, optionally calibrate moves), then `accumulate` (perform measurements) — or
 * the combined `warmup_and_accumulate` / `run`,
 * 4. call `collect_results` to reduce per-rank estimators across an `mpi::communicator`.
 *
 * The driver organises work into *cycles* of MC steps separated by measurement passes, and checks for user-requested
 * termination and for exceptions on configurable intervals so that ranks can stop in a coordinated way even with
 * asymmetric workloads.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-mc
 * @defgroup triqs-mc-utils MC utilities
 * @brief Various utilities used with @ref triqs-mc.
 *
 * @details This group collects auxiliary building blocks used by the MC framework, most prominently the random number
 * generator:
 * - triqs::mc_tools::random_generator is a type-erased wrapper around a pseudo-random engine. The engine is selected at
 * construction by name (`"mt19937"`, several `"lagged_fibonacci..."` variants, `"ranlux3"`, and the built-in Mersenne
 * Twister); samples are produced in batches into an internal buffer for efficiency. Overloaded `operator()` returns
 * either a uniform `double` in \f$ [0,1) \f$ or a uniform integer in \f$ \{0, ..., i-1 \} \f$, and the generator state
 * can be serialised to HDF5 for reproducible restarts.
 *
 * The list of available engine names is exposed at runtime via triqs::mc_tools::random_generator_names_list.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-meshes Meshes
 * @brief Various meshes for @ref triqs-gfs.
 *
 * @details Meshes form the basis for the @ref triqs-gfs (GF) containers in the **TRIQS** library. They determine the
 * mathematical domain of the underlying function, how it is represented, i.e. what is actually stored in memory, and
 * how it is evaluated.
 *
 * Every mesh satisfies the triqs::mesh::Mesh, and optionally the triqs::mesh::MeshWithValues concept. It consists of
 * \f$ N \f$ mesh points, where \f$ N \f$ is the size of the mesh. The mesh points themselves satisfy the
 * triqs::mesh::MeshPoint concept and they have two indices associated with it, as well as an optional value:
 * - The data index \f$ d \f$ is usually an integer \f$ \in \{ 0, \dots, N-1 \} \f$ (except for product meshes, for
 * which it is a tuple of indices) and simply indexes all mesh points in the mesh. The name refers to the fact that the
 * GF containers store a certain value/object for each mesh point in an array and the data index \f$ d \f$ is the
 * position of the value/object in that array.
 * - For some meshes, it is natural to associate each mesh point with another index \f$ n \f$. The meaning of the index
 * depends on the mesh type. For example,
 *   - for a triqs::mesh::imfreq mesh on the imaginary frequency axis, \f$ n \in \mathbb{Z} \f$ denotes the index of the
 *   Matsubara frequency \f$ i \omega_n \f$.
 *   - for a 3D triqs::mesh::cyclat mesh with periodic boundary conditions, \f$ n \equiv \mathbf{n} \in \mathbb{Z}^3 \f$
 *   denotes the index of the lattice point \f$ \mathbf{R}^{\mathbf{n}} \f$.
 *   - for a triqs::mesh::imtime mesh, the index is simply equal to its data index, i.e. \f$ n = d \f$.
 * - If a mesh satisfies the triqs::mesh::MeshWithValues concept, each mesh point has an additional value. For example,
 * the value of the mesh point with index \f$ n \f$
 *   - of a triqs::mesh::imfreq mesh is the Matsubara frequency \f$ i \omega_n \f$.
 *   - of a triqs::mesh::cyclat mesh is the lattice vector \f$ \mathbf{R}^{\mathbf{n}} \f$ in the standard basis.
 *   - of a triqs::mesh::imtime mesh is an imaginary time point \f$ \tau_n \in [0, \beta] \f$.
 *
 * To make this more concrete, let us look at a typical example. One of the most important quantities in quantum
 * many-body physics is the single particle GF \f$ G(\tau) \f$ defined on the imaginary time axis interval \f$ [0,
 * \beta] \f$. For simplicity, we assume that the GF is real valued such that \f$ G : [0, \beta] \in \mathbb{R} \to
 * \mathbb{R} \f$. The @ref triqs-gfs containers allow us to represent the same function with different meshes. For
 * example, we can use
 * - a triqs::mesh::imtime mesh on the imaginary time axis with \f$ N \f$ mesh points. In this case, the interval
 * \f$ [0, \beta] \f$ is divided into \f$ N \f$ equally spaced points \f$ \tau_n = n \beta / (N - 1) \f$, where the
 * imaginary time point \f$ \tau_n \f$ is the value of the mesh point with index \f$ n = d \in \{ 0, \dots, N-1 \} \f$.
 * For each mesh point, the GF container stores the function value \f$ G(\tau_n) \f$ in an array of size \f$ N \f$. To
 * evaluate the GF at an arbitrary \f$ \tau \in [0, \beta] \f$ with \f$ \tau_n \leq \tau \leq \tau_{n+1} \f$, the
 * container uses linear interpolation between the two mesh points \f$ \tau_n \f$ and \f$ \tau_{n+1} \f$.
 * - a triqs::mesh::legendre mesh with \f$ N \f$ mesh points. In this case, the mesh point with index \f$ n = d \in
 * \{ 0, \dots, N-1 \} \f$ refers to the Legendre polynomial \f$ P_n(x) \f$ of degree \f$ n \f$ and has no value
 * associated with it. The GF container stores the coefficients
 * \f[
 *   g_n = \sqrt{2n + 1} \int_0^\beta d\tau \, G(\tau) P_n(x(\tau)) \; ,
 * \f]
 * where \f$ x(\tau) = 2\tau / \beta - 1 \f$ is a linear map from \f$ [0, \beta] \f$ to \f$ [-1, 1] \f$. To evaluate
 * the GF at an arbitrary point \f$ \tau \in [0, \beta] \f$, the container uses a truncated series expansion of \f$
 * G(\tau) \f$ in terms of Legendre polynomials:
 * \f[
 *   G(\tau) \approx \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} \frac{\sqrt{2n + 1}}{\beta} g_n P_n(x(\tau)) \; .
 * \f]
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-meshes
 * @defgroup triqs-meshes-concepts Mesh concepts
 * @brief Various concepts used with @ref triqs-meshes.
 */

/**
