/**
 * @defgroup triqs-ops Operators
 * @brief Tools to construct and manipulate second-quantized many-body operators.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-ops module provides a symbolic algebra for second-quantized many-body operators acting on the
 * fermionic Fock space introduced in @ref triqs-hilbert.
 *
 * The central type is triqs::operators::many_body_operator (with the real- and complex-valued aliases
 * triqs::operators::many_body_operator_real and triqs::operators::many_body_operator_complex). A many-body operator
 * \f$ \hat{O} = \sum_i a_i \hat{m}_i \f$ is a linear combination of monomials \f$ \hat{m}_i \f$ (see
 * triqs::operators::monomial_t), where each monomial is an ordered product of canonical creation/annihilation
 * operators (see triqs::operators::canonical_ops_t). The operators form an algebra over the field of real/complex
 * scalars, with an additional addition operation between operators and scalars.
 *
 * Operators are not constructed directly but assembled from the factory functions triqs::operators::c (annihilation),
 * triqs::operators::c_dag (creation) and triqs::operators::n (number), combined with the usual `+`, `-` and `*`
 * arithmetic.
 *
 * Helper functions include `dagger` (Hermitian conjugation), triqs::operators::real / triqs::operators::imag
 * (real/imaginary parts of the coefficients), `transform` (apply a callable to each coefficient) and
 * triqs::operators::is_op_hermitian.
 *
 * The module is split into two parts:
 * - @ref triqs-ops-mbo — the many-body operator algebra itself.
 * - @ref triqs-ops-extractors — helpers that decompose an operator into coefficient tensors and matrices.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-ops
 * @defgroup triqs-ops-mbo Many-body operators
 * @brief The many-body operator algebra in second quantization.
 *
 * @details This group contains triqs::operators::many_body_operator_generic and its real- and complex-valued aliases.
 * An operator is stored as a map from monomials (triqs::operators::monomial_t, an ordered product of
 * triqs::operators::canonical_ops_t) to scalar coefficients. 
 * 
 * In addition the group provides:
 * 
 * - the creation/annihilation/number factories triqs::operators::c, triqs::operators::c_dag and triqs::operators::n,
 * - operator-operator and operator-scalar arithmetic (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`) implementing the algebra,
 * - Hermitian conjugation via `dagger` and Hermiticity checks via triqs::operators::is_op_hermitian,
 * - coefficient manipulation through `transform`, triqs::operators::real and triqs::operators::imag, and
 * - HDF5 serialization of operators together with a fundamental operator set.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-ops
 * @defgroup triqs-ops-extractors Operator utilities
 * @brief Helpers to extract coefficient tensors and maps from many-body operators.
 *
 * @details This group collects free functions that decompose a many-body operator of a known structure into coefficient 
 * dictionaries and dense arrays:
 * 
 * - utils::extract_h_dict, utils::extract_U_dict2 and utils::extract_U_dict4 read the coefficients of normal-ordered 
 * quadratic, density-density and general two-particle operators into index-keyed dictionaries.
 * - utils::dict_to_matrix and utils::dict_to_variant_matrix convert such dictionaries into dense arrays indexed by the 
 * linear indices of a triqs::hilbert_space::fundamental_operator_set.
 * - utils::quadratic_terms and utils::quartic_terms keep only the monomials of a given length. 
 * - utils::block_matrix_from_op and utils::op_from_block_matrix convert between a block-diagonal quadratic operator and 
 * its block-matrix representation.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-stat Statistical analysis tools
 * @brief Tools for the statistical analysis of Monte Carlo data and other sampled observables.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-stat module is a small, header-mostly toolkit for analysing samples produced by Monte Carlo
 * (or any other) simulations. It is built around two ideas: *streaming* accumulators that update mean, error and
 * autocorrelation estimates on the fly without storing the full sample stream, and *standalone* functions that operate
 * on a range of pre-collected samples.
 *
 * Every component has an MPI-aware variant — either an `*_mpi` overload (e.g. triqs::stat::mean_mpi,
 * triqs::stat::jackknife_mpi) or a member function taking an `mpi::communicator` (e.g.
 * triqs::stat::lin_binning::mean_error_and_tau, triqs::stat::log_binning::mean_errors_and_taus). The same client code
 * therefore runs serially or in parallel, and per-rank partial results are combined into a single estimator on all
 * ranks.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-stat-accs Accumulators
 * @ingroup triqs-stat
 * @brief Streaming binning accumulators that estimate mean, error and autocorrelation time on the fly.
 *
 * @details An *accumulator* receives samples one at a time via `operator<<` and maintains running estimators without
 * storing the full sample stream. Two flavours are provided:
 * - triqs::stat::lin_binning groups consecutive samples into equally sized bins. The bin data is kept and can be
 * accessed after the run, which makes it the natural input for downstream resampling (see
 * @ref triqs-stat-resampling). It can run either with a fixed maximum number of bins (bins are compressed by a factor
 * of two and the bin capacity is doubled whenever the maximum is reached) or with an unbounded number of bins of fixed
 * capacity.
 * - triqs::stat::log_binning is the canonical *autocorrelation* diagnostic: it maintains one bin per power-of-two bin
 * size and reports the standard error \f$ s_n \f$ and integrated autocorrelation time \f$ \tau_n \f$ for each binning
 * level. \f$ s_n \f$ and \f$ \tau_n \f$ are expected to grow with the bin size and saturate once the bin size exceeds
 * the autocorrelation time of the underlying chain.
 *
 * Both accumulators provide MPI-aware variants of their estimator methods and HDF5 serialisation for checkpointing long
 * runs. The free function triqs::stat::compress_bins merges adjacent bins of a `std::vector<T>` by a given
 * compression factor and is used by triqs::stat::lin_binning when aligning bin capacities across MPI ranks.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-stat-concepts Concepts
 * @ingroup triqs-stat
 * @brief C++20 concepts that constrain the value and range types accepted by @ref triqs-stat.
 *
 * @details The statistics module is generic in its sample type: it accepts plain scalars as well as `nda` arrays, but
 * only for a restricted set of element types. The three concepts in this group spell out those restrictions:
 * - triqs::stat::StatCompatible — a single sample value. Either a `double` / `std::complex<double>` scalar, or an
 * `nda::Array` whose value type is `double` / `std::complex<double>` and whose algebra is `'A'` (i.e. it behaves as a
 * generic array under elementwise operations).
 * - triqs::stat::AccCompatible — additionally required by the streaming accumulators (triqs::stat::lin_binning,
 * triqs::stat::log_binning). It refines triqs::stat::StatCompatible by also requiring scalars or *regular* array types,
 * so that bins can own their storage.
 * - triqs::stat::StatCompatibleRange — a sized forward range whose value type is triqs::stat::StatCompatible. This is
 * the type of input accepted by triqs::stat::mean, triqs::stat::mean_and_err and triqs::stat::jackknife.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-stat-meanerr Mean and error estimation
 * @ingroup triqs-stat
 * @brief Free functions that compute the mean / sum and a corresponding error estimate from a range of samples.
 *
 * @details Given a range of samples \f$ \{ x_i : i = 1, \ldots, N \} \f$, this group provides:
 * - triqs::stat::mean and its MPI-aware counterpart triqs::stat::mean_mpi, which return either the simple sum \f$
 * \sum_i x_i \f$ or the arithmetic mean \f$ \overline{x} \f$ depending on the triqs::stat::mean_tag template parameter.
 * - triqs::stat::mean_and_err and triqs::stat::mean_and_err_mpi, which return both the mean / sum and an error
 * estimate. The flavour of error is selected through the triqs::stat::error_tag enum: sum of squared deviations from
 * the mean, variance of the data, variance of the mean, standard error of the data, standard error of the mean, or
 * jackknife error.
 * - triqs::stat::tau_estimate_from_errors, which computes an estimate of the integrated autocorrelation time \f$ \tau
 * = \frac{1}{2} (s_n^2 / s_0^2 - 1) \f$ from a binned and an unbinned standard error.
 *
 * The two MPI variants accept a `std::optional<mpi::communicator>` and fall back to the serial implementation when no
 * communicator is provided; otherwise per-rank partial results are combined with a numerically careful reweighting so
 * that the reduced mean and sum of squared deviations match what a serial run on the concatenated data would have
 * produced.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-stat-resampling Resampling techniques
 * @ingroup triqs-stat
 * @brief Resampling algorithms for bias-corrected estimates of functions of averages.
 *
 * @details Many physical observables of interest are *non-linear* functions of statistical averages, for which the
 * naive estimate \f$ f(\overline{x}_1, \overline{x}_2, \ldots) \f$ carries a bias of order \f$ 1/N \f$ that has to be
 * corrected.
 *
 * triqs::stat::jackknife performs the delete-one jackknife resampling on one or more sample ranges and returns a tuple
 * containing:
 * - the bias-corrected estimate \f$ f_{\mathrm{corr}} = N \overline{f} - (N - 1) \overline{f}_{\mathrm{JK}} \f$,
 * - the jackknife error estimate \f$ s_f \f$,
 * - the jackknife function estimate \f$ \overline{f}_{\mathrm{JK}} \f$, and
 * - the naive estimate \f$ \overline{f} = f(\overline{x}_1, \overline{x}_2, \ldots) \f$.
 *
 * The MPI-aware variant triqs::stat::jackknife_mpi performs the same calculation on samples distributed across multiple
 * ranks; results are made available on all ranks. Both functions accept any callable and any number of
 * triqs::stat::StatCompatibleRange inputs.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-stat-utils Utilities
 * @ingroup triqs-stat
 * @brief Histograms and small helpers shared by the rest of the @ref triqs-stat module.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-test-tools Test tools
 * @brief GoogleTest-based helpers for writing TRIQS C++ unit tests.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-test-tools module is a header-only collection of helpers, built on top of **GoogleTest**, 
 * that the TRIQS C++ test suite uses to compare its core data structures and to set up test executables.
 * 
 * It provides various macros and helper functions to simplify writing unit tests involving TRIQS components.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup triqs-utility Utilities
 * @brief General-purpose utilities used across the **TRIQS** library.
 *
 * @details The @ref triqs-utility module collects foundational building blocks that the rest of the **TRIQS** library
 * depends on: compiler glue and exception types, template-metaprogramming helpers, numerical helpers (Legendre,
 * Chebyshev, Padé, ...), runtime utilities (timers, signal handling, crash logging) and a small set of I/O streams.
 *
 * The utilities are organised into the following thematic subgroups:
 * - @ref triqs-utility-io — reporting and debugging output streams,
 * - @ref triqs-utility-macros — foundational macros, exception types and compiler/platform glue,
 * - @ref triqs-utility-math — numerical and mathematical helpers,
 * - @ref triqs-utility-other — other general-purpose utilities,
 * - @ref triqs-utility-runtime — timers, signal handling and other runtime utilities,
 * - @ref triqs-utility-tuple — helpers for manipulating and serializing tuples and variants,
 * - @ref triqs-utility-traits — type traits and template-metaprogramming helpers.
 *
 * Most utilities live in the `triqs::utility` namespace.
 */

/**
 * @ingroup triqs-utility
